Subject: A constant range that runs past its buffer's pages is loaded with what is there

ir3 pushes a stage's constant ranges in whole vec4 and padded: a 16-byte colour
is a range of 64 bytes. The command processor reads all of it, the function
only what it uses, and Metal has the draw drawn whatever length was bound. A
range that ran past the bound object's pages was a refusal -- on the phone
"fixed_frag_lph_cph reads 64 bytes at 0 of constant buffer 2; 16 are bound and
16 mapped behind the offset" and "downsample_blur_vert_lph reads 64 bytes at 0
of constant buffer 3; 48 are bound and 48 mapped", each a layer or a blur stage
left out of a frame whose command buffer still ended Completed.

Such a range is now loaded direct: the bytes that are there, read when the
draw is encoded (as a range bound off 16 bytes already is), and zeros after
them. The command processor is still never let read past the pages. A draw
with such a range is not taken from its program's kept state (fastRanges
declines it) and is written whole. The same for a dispatch's ranges. A buffer
mapped nowhere for this processor is still refused, by name.

The refusal's two tests now expect the work done: the draw's constants are the
colour that was bound and zeros, first in its pass or after draws of the same
program from a kept state; the dispatch completes. Their golden lines change
with them, and no other line.


